{
  "id": "second-order-to-system",
  "subject_id": "mat2b",
  "chapter_number": 4,
  "week": 9,
  "title": "Second-Order ODE as a First-Order System",
  "eyebrow": "ODE systems",
  "language": "en",
  "duration": 60,
  "topic": "A linear second-order ODE y'' + p y' + q y = 0 becomes a first-order system in two unknowns by introducing v = y'. The state vector (y, v) evolves under a single 2×2 matrix, so every method built for first-order systems — Euler, RK4, eigenvector analysis — applies unchanged.",
  "concepts": [
    "second-order ODE",
    "first-order system",
    "state vector",
    "companion matrix",
    "phase plane"
  ],
  "pairs_with": [
    "chapter_concepts: system av differensialligninger",
    "chapter_concepts: tilstandsvektor"
  ],
  "prerequisites": [
    "euler-step"
  ],
  "beats": [
    {
      "id": "manimoIntro",
      "start": 0,
      "end": 9.66,
      "narration": "Why do we keep rewriting second-order equations as first-order systems? Because every numerical method, every matrix tool, is built for first order.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "center",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "manimo-enter",
            "content": "Manimo bobs centre",
            "delay": 0
          },
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "Hook caption.",
            "delay": 0.7,
            "color": "var(--chalk-100)",
            "position": "right of Manimo"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "secondOrder",
      "start": 9.66,
      "end": 21.83,
      "narration": "Start with a linear second-order equation. y double prime plus p times y prime plus q times y equals zero. One unknown function, two derivatives in play.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "centered formula",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "y'' + p y' + q y = 0",
            "delay": 0.4,
            "color": "var(--chalk-100)",
            "position": "centre"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "Linear, homogeneous, constant coefficients.",
            "delay": 1.8,
            "color": "var(--chalk-300)",
            "position": "below formula"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "introduceV",
      "start": 21.83,
      "end": 33.91,
      "narration": "Introduce a second unknown — call it v, and define it to be y prime. Then v prime is y double prime, which the equation tells us equals minus p v minus q y.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "two-line derivation",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "v = y'  ⇒  v' = y''",
            "delay": 0.4,
            "color": "var(--violet-400)",
            "position": "centre"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "v' = −p v − q y",
            "delay": 2,
            "color": "var(--amber-400)",
            "position": "below"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "matrixForm",
      "start": 33.91,
      "end": 50.09,
      "narration": "Stack y and v into a state vector. Its time derivative is a two by two matrix times itself. A second-order scalar equation has become a first-order vector equation — ready for Euler, R K four, or eigenvector analysis.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "matrix display",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "text-formula",
            "content": "(y, v)' = A · (y, v) with A = [[0,1],[-q,-p]]",
            "delay": 0.4,
            "color": "var(--chalk-100)",
            "position": "centre"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "Companion matrix.",
            "delay": 2.2,
            "color": "var(--chalk-200)",
            "position": "right"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "heroOutro",
      "start": 50.09,
      "end": 60,
      "narration": "One scalar second-order equation becomes a first-order system in twice as many unknowns. Same dynamics — friendlier toolbox.",
      "visuals": {
        "layout": "center",
        "elements": [
          {
            "type": "text-eyebrow",
            "content": "'COMPANION FORM'",
            "delay": 0,
            "color": "var(--amber-300)",
            "position": "centre-top"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-hero",
            "content": "'Order n scalar = order one system in n unknowns.'",
            "delay": 0.35,
            "color": "var(--chalk-100)",
            "position": "centre"
          },
          {
            "type": "text-caption",
            "content": "One trick — every method opens up.",
            "delay": 1.4,
            "color": "var(--chalk-200)",
            "position": "below hero"
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
